From 521cd66dfa6ecd2bf84403881738430c4b8ab554 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(interaction): signal sustained Bedrock item use

Mark food and other charge or consume actions as starting item use in player input. Let the Bedrock server authoritatively update consumed or replacement stacks, matching the captured native air-use transaction shape.

Decode protocol-2193 COMPLETED_USING_ITEM, whose signed little-endian item
ID and use-method fields are confirmed by the matching Mojang schema and
strict-BDS captures. Translate matching held-item completion to Java's
USE_ITEM_COMPLETE entity event. Preserve authoritative inventory counts
and ignore a completion for a replaced selected item.

The previously discarded completion leaves Java consumption state out of
phase with BDS and causes corrections during repeated eating. Verify this
boundary separately from bow charging and release. Packet tests exercise
signed IDs, use methods, replacement protection, and unchanged counts.

Matching build 1.26.51.1 executable inspection traces packet 142 from
its ID/name vtable through factory dispatcher 0x141734170 (handler slot
0x4b8) to ClientNetworkHandler 0x141306470. The handler looks up the
short item ID and rejects unsigned use methods above 16 before emitting
its event. Apply the same method range to Java translation so malformed
acknowledgments cannot complete a valid active use. Boundary packet tests
cover negative values, 16, 17, and signed integer extremes. This does not
resolve local prediction or late acknowledgments for a new same-item use.

Use the advertised native food component and current hunger limits to
admit StartUsingItem input. Preserve the air-use transaction when hunger
refuses food. Native 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 sends that transaction without
the start flag, including bread and component food at full hunger.
The earlier interpretation incorrectly suppressed the transaction too.

Retain ordered food component updates by connection registry identity.
Preserve can_always_eat and creative use; do not infer food behavior from
the mapped Java item or a fixed whitelist. Leave items without advertised
food data on their existing route. The captured 1.26.51.1 registry contains
this property for component and legacy foods, including apple, honey,
chorus fruit and both golden apple variants.

Targeted model and real packet controls verify refused-use transactions
without input flags or inventory writes, changed hunger maxima, always
edible food, creative use and omitted/replaced registry definitions.
The source format is also documented by Microsoft's minecraft:food item
component reference. Full custom item use-cycle and rejection/prediction
reconciliation remain separate requirements.

Keep unsupported offhand use on its existing container-resynchronization
path. The new eligibility check applies to the main-hand use transaction.
Both raw hand paths have targeted packet controls; neither changes the
canonical selected stack or queues a rejected use flag.

Ignore hunger for ordinary food on the server-confirmed Peaceful difficulty,
independently of the item's always-edible flag. Read current game-session
difficulty, including live server updates, before admitting the request.
Java client integration must also admit its local consumable predicate.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 controls on protocol 2225 refuse
full-hunger bread on Normal, consume 16->15 after switching to Peaceful,
and refuse after switching back. The old installed add-on retains 16 and
never enters use on Peaceful. Registered packet tests now carry those
difficulty transitions through the food admission boundary without
predicting any inventory consumption.

Compose food admission with installed use modifiers on component Item
receivers. Native 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 controls refuse food-only use at
full hunger, but consume while hungry and stop at twenty. Adding use
modifiers permits full-hunger use with either start_using mode, including
zero duration. The native food callback leaves the result unchanged when
hunger refuses it, so another installed handler can still start use.

Retain the advertised can_always_eat flag independently of that composed
admission. Transport effective full-hunger eligibility through Java FOOD,
including native nutrition and final saturation, so Java's local predicate
and outgoing use admission agree. Project component-food use
animation and network duration into CONSUMABLE, including zero duration,
so custom paper carriers also enter the local use cycle. Consume effects
remain server-owned. Preserve initial and current Peaceful exceptions and
server ownership of inventory consumption. Mark admitted food use in auth
input even when the definition has no optional minecraft:is_food tag.
Rejected and unsupported offhand requests do not gain that flag.

Registry and packet regressions cover installed component retention,
constructor-specific admission, full/hungry transitions and FOOD wire
properties without predicting stack consumption. Competing non-food use
handlers, complete custom consumable behavior, exact cycle timing, and the
original intermittent eating report remain separate requirements.

Official 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 held-use controls preserve the refused
air payload and continue block mode. Already admitted air mode still
sends retries after hunger becomes full. Registered packet regressions
cover both payload presence and absent StartUsingItem without changing
the canonical held count or offhand resynchronization.
